  "intro": "A linear equation in one variable can be solved by using inverse operations to isolate the variable: undo addition/subtraction, then multiplication/division, and distribute or combine like terms first. An equation can have one solution, no solution (variables cancel leaving a false statement), or infinitely many solutions (an identity). On the SAT, translate words into an equation, then solve carefully and check.",
  "items": [
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 1,
      "prompt": "Solve $3x-7=11$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Add $7$ to both sides: $3x=18$.",
          "Divide both sides by $3$: $x=6$.",
          "Check: $3(6)-7=18-7=11$."
        ],
        "answer": "$x=6$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 2,
      "prompt": "Solve $2(x+3)=4x-2$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Distribute: $2x+6=4x-2$.",
          "Subtract $2x$: $6=2x-2$; add $2$: $8=2x$.",
          "Divide by $2$: $x=4$."
        ],
        "answer": "$x=4$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 3,
      "prompt": "How many solutions does $2x+4=2x-5$ have?",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Subtract $2x$ from both sides: $4=-5$.",
          "This is a false statement with no variable left.",
          "So the equation has no solution."
        ],
        "answer": "No solution"
      }
    },
